<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/1st-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Matins of Bruges</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">18 May 1302</span> · Also known as Bruges Matins · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-flemish-war-1297-1305">Franco-Flemish War (1297–1305)</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Matins of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bruges">Bruges</a> was the uprising in Bruges before dawn on 18 May 1302 in which Flemish townsmen led by Pieter de Coninck and Jan Breydel massacred the French garrison installed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-iv-of-france">Philip IV of France</a>. It began the Flemish revolt that led to the defeat of the French knights by Flemish militias at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-golden-spurs">Battle of the Golden Spurs</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kortrijk">Kortrijk</a> on 11 July 1302.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/matins-of-bruges.jpg" alt="Matins of Bruges" width="320" height="146" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown sculptor, Chest of Courtrai · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brugse_metten.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
